  "rules.html": "<h1>How to play</h1><h2>Goal</h2><p>Undercover Responsible AI is a <strong>serious game</strong>: by unmasking the practice that breaks the rule, you <strong>learn or recall the 7 golden rules</strong> of responsible AI, and you <strong>challenge your current practices</strong> by holding them up against these rules.</p><h2>Setting up</h2><ol><li>The facilitator creates a game: players' names, golden rules to play, number of rounds and cards (the validated base deck, or the base deck with the expansion, whose cards drawn from insurance jobs are still to be validated). From 7 players, they can plan 2 undercovers, with the same card or a different card each.</li><li>They show the <strong>QR code</strong> (or the game code).</li><li>Everyone scans the QR code and selects <strong>their name</strong>, or picks a <strong>seat number</strong> if no names were entered before starting the game. Each name or number must be taken only once. Participants are not checked automatically, to keep the game as simple as possible.</li><li>On the participant's device (phone or computer), the card dealt for the current round is shown face down: turn it over by tapping it, clicking it or swiping it, or with the “Show my card” button. The same gesture hides it again.</li></ol><p class=\"hint\">No connection between phones is needed: each phone works out its own card from the code.</p><h2>Two game modes</h2><p>In both modes, the <strong>teammates</strong> have a good practice linked to the round's golden rule; the <strong>undercover</strong> has a card on the same subject that <em>breaks</em> the rule.</p><h3>Cooperative (recommended)</h3><ol><li><strong>The app does not reveal any roles</strong>. Whoever has the undercover card may recognise it when reading it: they still read it word for word, like everyone else.</li><li><strong>Reading</strong>: starting with the chosen player, everyone reads their card aloud, <strong>word for word</strong>. A summarised or reworded card skews the whole team's investigation.</li><li>Timed <strong>debate</strong> (1 minute by default): which practice breaks the golden rule, and why? Quote the exact sentence from the card before judging.</li><li><strong>Naming a suspect</strong>: the team names a suspect. If they do not have the undercover card, they are cleared and you start again.</li><li><strong>No one is to blame</strong>: being named, or having the undercover card, is nobody's fault. Nobody chooses their card: we discuss practices, not people.</li><li><strong>Points for the team</strong>: 3 points if the undercover is found on the first vote, 2 on the second, 1 on the third, 0 after that.</li></ol><h3>Competitive</h3><ol><li>When turning their card over, everyone discovers their role: <strong>the undercover knows they are the undercover</strong> and must pass themselves off as a teammate. You need at least 4 players: with 3, a single teammate wrongly eliminated loses the round.</li><li><strong>Speaking round</strong>: everyone may keep their card in front of them, but <strong>describes it in their own words</strong>, without reading it. Teammates describe it honestly: only the undercover may bluff.</li><li>Timed <strong>debate</strong>: anyone may ask a question to a player whose description seems unclear. Teammates look for the description that avoids a detail; the undercover talks about the context of their card and keeps quiet about what gives it away. A vague or exaggerated description proves nothing: a teammate may also describe their card poorly.</li><li><strong>Vote</strong>: the eliminated player reveals their role. If it is a teammate, the round goes on: new debate, new vote.</li><li>The <strong>teammates</strong> win the round if they eliminate the undercover; the <strong>undercover</strong> wins it if only one teammate is left.</li><li>The score counts the <strong>rounds won by each side</strong>.</li></ol><h2>Fairness</h2><p>From one round to the next, the app rotates the undercover role and the player who starts: the same player may get them twice in a row, but it is rare.</p><h2>End of the game</h2><p>After the last round, the facilitator shows the score and a QR code: by scanning it, each player finds the results on their phone. Tapping a round shows the cards that were dealt and who had them. If the game was ended early, the rounds not played can still be viewed, outside the score.</p><h2>Debrief</h2><p>At the end of each round, the facilitator shows the rule, the undercover card and why it breaks the rule. This is the moment to talk about the teams' real practices.</p>",

